---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import OnePlaceDashboard from '../../components/OnePlaceDashboard';
---
<Base title="One Place, A Thousand Questions" description="How to start a long-term biodiversity inventory in your backyard, a permitted woodland, or a familiar park—with practical iNaturalist guidance and a living example.">
<div class="op-page">
  <section class="op-hero">
    <div class="op-wrap op-hero-grid">
      <div><p class="op-kicker">DISCOVER / FIELDWORK YOU CAN START TODAY</p><h1>One place.<br/><em>A thousand questions.</em></h1><p class="op-intro">What might you discover if you kept returning to the same ordinary place—and kept paying attention?</p><div class="op-actions"><a class="op-button" href="#get-started">Start your own inventory ↓</a><a class="op-textlink" href="#example">See a living example ↗</a></div></div>
      <figure><img src="/images/field-photography/field-specimen-color-reference.jpg" alt="A wild mushroom documented carefully in the field with a scale and color reference" loading="eager"/><figcaption>Start small. Observe carefully. Return often.</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="op-section"><div class="op-wrap op-narrow"><p class="op-kicker">THE IDEA</p><h2>Science doesn't have to begin somewhere extraordinary.</h2><p>Maybe it's your yard. Maybe it's a stretch of woodland you have permission to visit, a public trail, a neighborhood park, or the same patch of city greenspace you walk past every morning. Pick a place you can return to. Learn its seasons. Start documenting the life you find there.</p><p>One visit offers a snapshot. Years of returning create a record: which organisms appear, when they return, how habitats change, and which questions keep getting more interesting.</p><p><strong>The goal isn't to win a species-counting contest.</strong> It's to discover how much one place can teach you—and to leave a useful record for the people who come after you.</p></div></section>
  <section class="op-section op-cream"><div class="op-wrap"><p class="op-kicker">FIND YOUR ONE PLACE</p><h2>It doesn't have to be yours.</h2><div class="op-types"><article><span>01 / CLOSE TO HOME</span><h3>A yard or garden</h3><p>Observe the insects on planted flowers, fungi in the lawn, birds overhead, lichens on trees, and everything growing where you least expect it.</p></article><article><span>02 / WITH PERMISSION</span><h3>A private woodland or farm</h3><p>Return to a site where the owner permits access. Agree on where you can go, whether collecting is allowed, and what location information can be shared.</p></article><article><span>03 / PUBLIC LAND</span><h3>A park, preserve, or trail</h3><p>Choose a repeatable route or defined study plot. Follow access rules and remember that permission to observe does not necessarily include permission to collect.</p></article></div></div></section>
  <section class="op-section" id="get-started"><div class="op-wrap"><p class="op-kicker">A PRACTICAL STARTING POINT</p><h2>Six steps. No special credentials.</h2><div class="op-steps">
    <article><b>01</b><div><h3>Choose and define your place.</h3><p>Choose an accessible site you can revisit. Write down its boundaries and stick to them. You can draw a polygon in Google Earth or My Maps and save a KML file, but a KML is optional at the beginning. For private property, keep exact boundaries private unless you intentionally want them public.</p></div></article>
    <article><b>02</b><div><h3>Start observing on iNaturalist.</h3><p>Photograph or record wild organisms, note the date and the organism's location, and add useful habitat or substrate details. Include fungi, plants, insects, birds, mosses, lichens, and organisms you cannot name. You can begin without creating an iNaturalist Project.</p></div></article>
    <article><b>03</b><div><h3>Decide what belongs in the inventory.</h3><p>Use the same geographic definition year after year. Distinguish planted organisms from wild ones, and flyovers from animals using the site. Repeat sightings are valuable. If the study area changes, document when and why.</p></div></article>
    <article><b>04</b><div><h3>Choose your project approach, if you want one.</h3><p><strong>Collection Projects</strong> automatically include observations matching defined filters and work well for suitable public areas. <strong>Traditional Projects</strong> let you manually curate observations and can be better for small residential sites, obscured observations, or boundaries that cannot be reliably captured by automatic filters. Traditional Projects do not auto-populate: add observations individually or in batches.</p></div></article>
    <article><b>05</b><div><h3>Return in different seasons.</h3><p>Visit after rain, during flowering, at night when appropriate, and across multiple years. Record methods or effort when you can—otherwise a growing species list may reflect more time spent looking rather than an ecological change.</p></div></article>
    <article><b>06</b><div><h3>Follow the questions.</h3><p>Ask what a species is growing on, what pollinates a flower, or why a mushroom appears after a certain weather pattern. Compare your notes, invite identification help, and pursue microscopy or sequencing when useful and lawful.</p></div></article>
  </div><div class="op-resource-links"><a href="https://www.inaturalist.org/observations/upload" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Start an iNaturalist observation ↗</a><a href="https://www.inaturalist.org/projects/new" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Explore iNaturalist Projects ↗</a><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/d/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Draw a study boundary in Google My Maps ↗</a></div></div></section>
  <section class="op-section op-dark" id="example"><div class="op-wrap"><p class="op-kicker">WHAT THIS LOOKS LIKE IN PRACTICE</p><h2>My own one-place experiment.</h2><p class="op-dark-lead">I started documenting fungi around a single residential property in Northeast Ohio. It isn't a public project to join or a destination to visit. It's an example of what sustained curiosity can produce in one familiar place.</p><OnePlaceDashboard client:visible /><p class="op-note">The counts reflect publicly searchable records in this fungi-only iNaturalist project, not every fungus on the property or every observation in my iNaturalist account. Search API totals can differ from the project's own overview. There is no public map of the residential study boundary here.</p></div></section>
  <section class="op-section"><div class="op-wrap op-outro"><div><p class="op-kicker">KEEP THE SCIENCE HONEST</p><h2>What you find is not everything that's there.</h2><p>Observation lists are shaped by weather, season, access, effort, detectability, equipment, and identification uncertainty. A record of 100 observed taxa doesn't mean a place contains only 100 taxa. If you want to compare sites—or years—record how and how often you searched.</p><p>On private land, secure permission before entering or collecting. On public land, check site-specific rules; many parks and preserves restrict specimen removal. Avoid publishing precise locations of sensitive species or private residential sites without consent.</p></div><aside><p>THE QUESTION TO KEEP</p><blockquote>What can I notice here today that I missed yesterday?</blockquote><a class="op-button" href="/learn/">Practice looking closer →</a></aside></div></section>
</div>
</Base>
